  'live', 0, 0, '[]', u.id, unixepoch() * 1000, unixepoch() * 1000
FROM (VALUES
  ('PST-0207', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Ayub and Yahya', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Tashkent Declaration of 1966 was signed for Pakistan by:', '["Liaquat Ali Khan", "Zulfikar Ali Bhutto", "Yahya Khan", "Ayub Khan"]', 3, 'President Ayub Khan and Indian PM Lal Bahadur Shastri signed it, hosted by Soviet Premier Kosygin.', '{"0": "Liaquat died in 1951.", "1": "Bhutto was Foreign Minister and later broke with Ayub over it.", "2": "Yahya was not head of state then."}'),
  ('PST-0208', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Ayub and Yahya', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Pakistan Peoples Party was founded in 1967 by:', '["Zulfikar Ali Bhutto", "Asghar Khan", "Mumtaz Daultana", "Benazir Bhutto"]', 0, 'Bhutto founded the PPP in Lahore on 30 November 1967; its slogan was ''Roti, Kapra aur Makan''.', '{"1": "Asghar Khan founded Tehrik-e-Istiqlal.", "2": "Daultana was a Muslim League leader.", "3": "Benazir led the party later."}'),
  ('PST-0209', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Ayub and Yahya', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Legal Framework Order (LFO) of 1970, which set the rules for the 1970 elections, was issued by:', '["Yahya Khan", "Zulfikar Ali Bhutto", "Pervez Musharraf", "Ayub Khan"]', 0, 'Yahya Khan''s LFO of 30 March 1970 also ended One Unit.', '{"1": "Bhutto was a party leader then.", "2": "Musharraf''s LFO came in 2002.", "3": "Ayub resigned in 1969."}'),
  ('PST-0210', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Ayub and Yahya', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'In the 1970 elections, the party that won a majority in East Pakistan was the:', '["Awami League", "Muslim League", "Pakistan Peoples Party", "Jamaat-e-Islami"]', 0, 'Sheikh Mujib''s Awami League won 160 of 162 East Pakistan seats.', '{"1": "The League won few seats.", "2": "The PPP won most seats in West Pakistan.", "3": "Jamaat won few seats."}'),
  ('PST-0211', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Ayub and Yahya', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The armed Bengali force that fought alongside India in 1971 was called the:', '["Razakars", "Khudai Khidmatgar", "Mukti Bahini", "Al-Badr"]', 2, 'The Mukti Bahini (''liberation force'') fought the Pakistan Army in East Pakistan.', '{"0": "Razakars sided with the Pakistan Army.", "1": "That was a Pakhtun movement before 1947.", "3": "Al-Badr sided with the Pakistan Army."}'),
  ('PST-0212', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'At the Lahore Islamic Summit of February 1974, Pakistan formally:', '["Tested a nuclear device", "Joined the Commonwealth", "Recognised Bangladesh", "Left CENTO"]', 2, 'Pakistan recognised Bangladesh on 22 February 1974, and Sheikh Mujib attended the summit.', '{"0": "That was in 1998.", "1": "Pakistan had left the Commonwealth in 1972.", "3": "Pakistan left CENTO in 1979."}'),
  ('PST-0213', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Pakistan Economic Survey; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'Pakistan Steel Mills at Karachi was built with the help of:', '["Japan", "The Soviet Union", "China", "The United States"]', 1, 'The Soviet Union helped build Pakistan Steel at Bin Qasim in the 1970s.', '{"0": "Japan did not build it.", "2": "China helped with other projects, like Heavy Mechanical Complex.", "3": "The USA did not build it."}'),
  ('PST-0214', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Pakistan Economic Survey; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Karachi Nuclear Power Plant (KANUPP), Pakistan''s first, began working in 1972 with help from:', '["France", "Canada", "The United States", "China"]', 1, 'KANUPP was a Canadian-supplied CANDU reactor.', '{"0": "A French reprocessing deal was cancelled.", "2": "The USA did not build it.", "3": "China built the later Chashma plants."}'),
  ('PST-0215', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'General Zia-ul-Haq imposed martial law on:', '["5 July 1977", "7 October 1958", "25 March 1969", "12 October 1999"]', 0, 'Zia overthrew Zulfikar Ali Bhutto on 5 July 1977.', '{"1": "That was the first martial law (Iskander Mirza and Ayub).", "2": "That was Yahya Khan''s martial law.", "3": "That was Musharraf''s takeover."}'),
  ('PST-0216', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Hudood Ordinances were issued in:', '["1961", "1973", "1979", "1985"]', 2, 'Zia-ul-Haq issued the Hudood Ordinances in February 1979.', '{"0": "1961 is the Family Laws Ordinance.", "1": "1973 is when the present Constitution came into force.", "3": "1985 is the Eighth Amendment."}'),
  ('PST-0217', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'Muhammad Khan Junejo became Prime Minister in:', '["1977", "1990", "1985", "1988"]', 2, 'After the non-party elections of 1985, Zia appointed Junejo Prime Minister.', '{"0": "1977 was the coup year.", "1": "Nawaz Sharif became PM in 1990.", "3": "Junejo was dismissed in 1988."}'),
  ('PST-0218', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Ojhri Camp ammunition depot blew up in Rawalpindi on:', '["29 May 1988", "17 August 1988", "5 July 1977", "10 April 1988"]', 3, 'The Ojhri Camp blast killed about 100 people in Rawalpindi and Islamabad.', '{"0": "That is when Junejo was dismissed.", "1": "That is when Zia''s plane crashed.", "2": "That is the 1977 coup."}'),
  ('PST-0219', 'Pakistan since 1947', 'Bhutto and Zia', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The US Ambassador who died with General Zia in the plane crash of 17 August 1988 was:', '["Robert Oakley", "Henry Kissinger", "Dean Hinton", "Arnold Raphel"]', 3, 'Arnold Raphel was among those killed near Bahawalpur.', '{"0": "Oakley became ambassador after the crash.", "1": "Kissinger was US Secretary of State in the 1970s.", "2": "Hinton was ambassador earlier."}'),
  ('PST-0220', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'Benazir Bhutto''s first government was dismissed by President Ghulam Ishaq Khan in:', '["November 1996", "July 1977", "August 1990", "October 1999"]', 2, 'Ghulam Ishaq Khan dismissed her government on 6 August 1990 under Article 58(2)(b).', '{"0": "That was her second government, dismissed by Leghari.", "1": "July 1977 is when her father was overthrown.", "3": "That was Nawaz Sharif''s government."}'),
  ('PST-0221', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Commonwealth Secretariat', 'Pakistan rejoined the Commonwealth in:', '["1972", "2008", "1999", "1989"]', 3, 'Pakistan left in 1972 over Bangladesh and rejoined in 1989.', '{"0": "Pakistan left the Commonwealth in 1972.", "1": "Pakistan''s suspension was lifted in 2008.", "2": "Pakistan was suspended in 1999."}'),
  ('PST-0222', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf was founded by Imran Khan in:', '["1988", "2002", "1992", "1996"]', 3, 'Imran Khan founded PTI on 25 April 1996.', '{"0": "Imran was still playing cricket.", "1": "PTI contested the 2002 election but was founded earlier.", "2": "1992 was the year of the cricket World Cup win."}'),
  ('PST-0223', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'moderate', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'Tehrik-e-Istiqlal was founded by:', '["Maulana Maududi", "Air Marshal Asghar Khan", "Zulfikar Ali Bhutto", "Imran Khan"]', 1, 'Former air chief Asghar Khan founded Tehrik-e-Istiqlal in 1970.', '{"0": "Maududi founded Jamaat-e-Islami.", "2": "Bhutto founded the PPP.", "3": "Imran founded PTI."}'),
  ('PST-0224', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'After his takeover on 12 October 1999, General Pervez Musharraf first took the title of:', '["Prime Minister", "Chief Executive", "Governor-General", "Chief Martial Law Administrator"]', 1, 'Musharraf called himself Chief Executive and became President in June 2001.', '{"0": "He did not become Prime Minister.", "2": "That office ended in 1956.", "3": "He avoided this title, unlike Zia."}'),
  ('PST-0225', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The Agra Summit between Pervez Musharraf and Atal Bihari Vajpayee was held in:', '["July 1972", "February 1999", "July 2001", "January 2004"]', 2, 'The Agra Summit of 14–16 July 2001 ended without a joint statement.', '{"0": "July 1972 was the Simla Agreement.", "1": "February 1999 was the Lahore Declaration.", "3": "January 2004 was the Islamabad SAARC summit."}'),
  ('PST-0226', 'Pakistan since 1947', '1988 onwards', 'easy', 'Markhor Prep original question. HEC Law Admission Test (LAT) Part I, Pakistan Studies. Checked against: Ian Talbot, Pakistan: A New History; Pakistan Studies (compulsory), PTB', 'The first woman Speaker of the National Assembly of Pakistan was:', '["Dr Fehmida Mirza", "Benazir Bhutto", "Begum Ra''ana Liaquat Ali Khan", "Hina Rabbani Khar"]', 0, 'Dr Fehmida Mirza was elected Speaker in March 2008.', '{"1": "Benazir was the first woman Prime Minister.", "2": "She was the first woman Governor of Sindh.", "3": "Khar was the first woman Foreign Minister."}')
